structured_output.summary:
The report's call was fade, with p_runner 12 and p_rug 62, and it held up. Entry was at about $146k market cap. Price never gained more than 3.5%, fell 20% within 17 minutes and 50% within 54 minutes, and the trade stopped out at -52%. The main drivers the report identified were real and available at the time:
- the pool was deployed straight to PumpSwap with unlocked LP that the deployer controlled;
- the token was a name copy with no link to the project, and earlier copies were already dead;
- buying was lopsided (1,949 unique buyers against 53 unique sellers) and new-buyer arrival was fading;
- one wallet held 22.89% of supply.

The report missed one strong clue. Holders outside the top 40 held only 2.5% of supply, which shows the holder count was mostly dust wallets. Its counterargument also leaned on counts that are easy to inflate: holders and the buy/sell ratio.

Two things cap how much credit the call gets. First, price rose about 68% between data collection and entry. The -52% loss is mostly entry timing, and measured from the T2 price the exit was only about 17% lower. Second, the rug is not confirmed. The data shows zero exit liquidity at 6h and 24h, but it also reports minimum 24h liquidity of $60k and no liquidity collapse.

So the outcome was knowable in direction but not in timing or size. The reasoning was good, and it could have been more confident.
